[
  {
    "name": "paste_compound_shape_offsets_its_operands",
    "function": "paste_translate",
    "_comment": "THE DIVERGENCE. Pasting a live COMPOUND SHAPE applied the 24pt offset in Rust and NOT in Swift: Swift's paste helper sent everything that was not a group or a layer to `moveControlPoints`, whose compound arm is a no-op, so the pasted compound landed exactly on top of its source -- invisible, which is the one outcome the offset exists to prevent (workspace/actions.yaml \u00a7paste: 'offset 24 points down and to the right', against paste_in_place's explicit 'no offset'). The operands carry ids and names so the vector also pins that a translation speaks to POSITION only. THE COMPOUND ITSELF is named too, which it was not when this family landed: Swift's live conformers had no `name` property at all, so a named live element could not be expressed here without the vector reddening for a reason unrelated to its subject. That is fixed, and the name is now part of what a paste must carry.",
